==German==

===Etymology===
{{bor+|de|la|prō|t=for}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|de|De-pro.ogg|a=<<Germany>> (<<Berlin>>)}}

===Preposition===
{{head|de|preposition}} {{+obj|de|acc/dat/nom|catpos=prep}}

# [[per]], [[each]]
#: {{syn|de|je|für}}
#: {{ux|de|Samt Mehrwertsteuer ergibt sich ein Kaufpreis von rund 30 Euro '''pro''' Stück|After VAT the price comes to around 30 euros '''each'''.}}
#: {{ux|de|Der durchschnittliche '''Pro'''-Kopf-Konsum von Bier in Deutschland im Jahr 2018 summierte sich auf rund 101,1 Liter.|Average beer consumption in Germany in 2018 came to 101.1 liters '''per''' head.}}

====Usage notes====
* Followed by a noun in either the accusative, dative or nominative case. No semantic distinction is made between the cases here. Examples from Duden:
*: {{uxi|de|pro gefahren'''en''' / gefahren'''em''' Kilometer|per kilometer travelled}}
*: {{uxi|de|pro verkauft'''es''' / verkauft'''em''' Exemplar|for every copy sold}}
** The accusative is the most prescriptive and most formal sounding case.
** The nominative is especially popular when a bare noun (i.e. without adjectives or other modifiers) follows the preposition:
**: {{uxi|de|pro Mensch|-}} {{small|{{abbr|NOM|nominative}}}} >> {{uxi|de|pro Menschen|-}} {{small|{{abbr|ACC|accusative}}}} / {{small|{{abbr|DAT|dative}}}}
**: {{uxi|de|pro Kollege|-}} {{small|{{abbr|NOM|nominative}}}} >> {{uxi|de|pro Kollegen|-}} {{small|{{abbr|ACC|accusative}}}} / {{small|{{abbr|DAT|dative}}}}
* When used in a Latin phrase, the ablative is used according to the rules of Latin grammar: {{m|de|pars pro toto}} or ''Pars pro Toto'', and {{m|de|pro forma}} or ''pro Forma''.

